   "source": [
    "Matrix Representation: In this lab you will be creating a simple linear algebra system. In memory, we will represent matrices as nested python lists as we have done in lecture. In the exercises below, you are required to explicitly test every feature you implement, demonstrating it works.\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Create a `matrix` class with the following properties:\n",
    "    * It can be initialized in 2 ways:\n",
    "        1. with arguments `n` and `m`, the size of the matrix. A newly instanciated matrix will contain all zeros.\n",
    "        2. with a list of lists of values. Note that since we are using lists of lists to implement matrices, it is possible that not all rows have the same number of columns. Test explicitly that the matrix is properly specified.\n",
    "    * Matrix instances `M` can be indexed with `M[i][j]` and `M[i,j]`.\n",
    "    * Matrix assignment works in 2 ways:\n",
    "        1. If `M_1` and `M_2` are `matrix` instances `M_1=M_2` sets the values of `M_1` to those of `M_2`, if they are the same size. Error otherwise.\n",
    "        2. In example above `M_2` can be a list of lists of correct size.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "--- Initialized with Matrix(2, 3) ---\n",
      "[0, 0, 0]\n",
      "[0, 0, 0]\n",
      "\n",
      "--- Initialized with values ---\n",
      "[1, 2]\n",
      "[3, 4]\n",
      "\n",
      "--- Testing Access M[0,1] and M[0][1] ---\n",
      "Value at [0,1]: 2\n",
      "Value at [0][1]: 2\n",
      "\n",
      "--- After set_values ---\n",
      "[10, 20]\n",
      "[30, 40]\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "class Matrix:\n",
    "    def __init__(self, *args):\n",
    "        # Case 1: Initializing with n and m (e.g., Matrix(3, 4))\n",
    "        if len(args) == 2 and isinstance(args[0], int) and isinstance(args[1], int):\n",
    "            n, m = args\n",
    "            self._data = [[0 for _ in range(m)] for _ in range(n)]\n",
    "            self._rows = n\n",
    "            self._cols = m\n",
    "        \n",
    "        # Case 2: Initializing with a list of lists\n",
    "        elif len(args) == 1 and isinstance(args[0], list):\n",
    "            data = args[0]\n",
    "            if not data:\n",
    "                self._data = []\n",
    "                self._rows = 0\n",
    "                self._cols = 0\n",
    "                return\n",
    "            first_row_len = len(data[0])\n",
    "            for row in data:\n",
    "                if not isinstance(row, list) or len(row) != first_row_len:\n",
    "                    raise ValueError(\"Matrix improperly specified: rows have different lengths\")\n",
    "            \n",
    "            self._data = [list(row) for row in data]\n",
    "            self._rows = len(data)\n",
    "            self._cols = first_row_len\n",
    "        else:\n",
    "            raise TypeError(\"Invalid arguments for Matrix initialization\")\n",
    "\n",
    "    def __getitem__(self, index):\n",
    "        if isinstance(index, tuple):\n",
    "            i, j = index\n",
    "            return self._data[i][j]\n",
    "        return self._data[index]\n",
    "\n",
    "    def set_values(self, other):\n",
    "        \"\"\"\n",
    "        Logic for 'M1 = M2' behavior as described. \n",
    "        Note: In Python, '=' always rebinds names, so a method is used \n",
    "        to simulate copying values into an existing instance.\n",
    "        \"\"\"\n",
    "        if isinstance(other, Matrix):\n",
    "            other_data = other._data\n",
    "            o_rows, o_cols = other._rows, other._cols\n",
    "        elif isinstance(other, list):\n",
    "            o_rows = len(other)\n",
    "            o_cols = len(other[0]) if o_rows > 0 else 0\n",
    "            other_data = other\n",
    "        else:\n",
    "            raise TypeError(\"Can only assign from Matrix or list of lists\")\n",
    "\n",
    "        # Error if sizes are not the same\n",
    "        if o_rows != self._rows or o_cols != self._cols:\n",
    "            raise ValueError(\"Dimensions do not match for assignment\")\n",
    "\n",
    "        self._data = [list(row) for row in other_data]\n",
    "\n",
    "    def __str__(self):\n",
    "        return \"\\n\".join([str(row) for row in self._data])\n",
    "\n",
    "# 1. *args: This allows the __init__ to accept a variable number of arguments, \n",
    "# which we use to distinguish between the (n, m) case and the (list) case.\n",
    "\n",
    "# 2. index tuple: When we write M[i, j], Python passes (i, j) as a single \n",
    "# tuple argument to __getitem__. \n",
    "\n",
    "# 3. list(row): This is used to create a new copy of the list. If we just did \n",
    "# self._data = data, changing the original list would change our Matrix.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "m_zeros = Matrix(2, 3)\n",
    "print(\"--- Initialized with Matrix(2, 3) ---\")\n",
    "print(m_zeros)\n",
    "\n",
    "m_vals = Matrix([[1, 2], [3, 4]])\n",
    "print(\"\\n--- Initialized with values ---\")\n",
    "print(m_vals)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\n--- Testing Access M[0,1] and M[0][1] ---\")\n",
    "print(\"Value at [0,1]:\", m_vals[0, 1]) \n",
    "print(\"Value at [0][1]:\", m_vals[0][1])\n",
    "\n",
    "m_vals.set_values([[10, 20], [30, 40]])\n",
    "print(\"\\n--- After set_values ---\")\n",
    "print(m_vals)"
   ]
  },
